% ============================================================================
% speleothem-alt-name — the other names a cave formation goes by
% (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? speleothem_alt_name(coralloid, $N)
%       % corallite
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `speleothem_alt_name(speleothem, alt_name)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls. The query also runs BACKWARD, which is the direction that
% actually matters here: a reader who meets "organ pipes" on a cave tour
% needs to get back to `frozen_waterfall`, not the other way round.
%
% SEVENTH cave/karst library, and the first on the naming axis. Same shape
% as `astronomy/space-rock-alt-name.adj`, which reads its pairs from the
% same kind of apposition ("the fireballs or \"shooting stars\" are called
% meteors").
%
% Provenance: four sentences from the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Speleothems" page, one per name group.
%
%     speleothem         alt_name(s)                    grounding sentence
%     ----------------   ----------------------------   ------------------
%     column             pillar                         (1) formation
%     coralloid          corallite                      (2) catchall
%     frozen_waterfall   petrified_waterfall,           (3) flowstone forms
%                        cascades, rivers,
%                        glaciers, organ_pipes
%     drapery            cave_bacon_when_...            (4) bacon condition
%
% *** A LIMITATION OF THIS TABLE IN PARTICULAR, STATED UP FRONT. ***
% Provenance in this stdlib is attached at TABLE level: the `source`
% envelope and every `cites` corroboration cover EVERY row. Most tables
% here are grounded in one or two sentences, so that is harmless. This one
% is grounded in FOUR, covering four unrelated speleothems — so an answer
% about `column` also carries the coralloid, flowstone and bacon sentences,
% only one of which grounds it. In the JSON output nothing cited is false,
% and the grounding sentence is always among them, but the reader has to
% match sentence to row themselves.
%
% IN `--explain` OUTPUT IT IS SHARPER THAN THAT, and worth knowing before
% trusting that view. Explain renders only the PRIMARY `source` and drops
% corroborations entirely, so the `corallite` row is displayed under the
% column/pillar sentence — a sentence that does not ground it. That is a
% property of every multi-sentence table in this stdlib, not of this one:
% `heredity-term.adj` (6 corroborations, 7 rows), `bill-stage-successor.adj`
% (6, 7) and `checks-and-balances.adj` (4, 5) all behave the same way. It is
% filed rather than worked around here, because fixing it means changing
% how explain renders provenance for a dozen shipped libraries at once.
% Until then, THE JSON OUTPUT IS THE AUTHORITATIVE VIEW and `--explain` is
% a summary that can attribute a row to the wrong sentence.
%
% The alternative was four single-purpose tables, which would fragment one
% lookup ("what else is this called?") into four and make the backward
% query — the useful one — impossible to ask in a single goal. One table
% with a stated limitation is the better trade. Row-level provenance is
% filed as issue #13893; when it lands, this header should be revisited
% and the note deleted rather than left to rot.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the National Park Service is a first-party .gov
% publisher, the same source the six sibling cave/karst libraries cite.
% Every sentence below was taken from a RAW TEXT EXTRACTION of the page,
% not from a fetch summary. That distinction is not pedantry: summarised
% readings of this page disagreed with each other about how many times
% "bacon" occurs and whether a sentence existed at all, and one of them
% would have had this table assert a row the page contradicts (see the
% `cave_popcorn` abstention below).
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - CAVE_POPCORN IS NOT A ROW, AND THE REASON IS THAT THE SOURCE
%     CONTRADICTS ITSELF. Sentence (2) offers it as a synonym —
%     "Coralloid (or corallite or cave popcorn) is a catchall term" — but
%     a later sentence makes it a MEMBER of the category instead:
%     "Coralloids include cave popcorn, grapes, knobstone, coral,
%     cauliflower, globularites, and grapefruit." A thing cannot be both
%     another name for coralloids and one of several kinds of coralloid.
%     `corallite` has no such problem: it appears only in the
%     parenthetical, so it ships. Picking whichever reading suited the
%     table would be choosing an answer and then finding a citation for
%     it, which is the exact failure this stdlib exists to prevent.
%   - BARE `cave_bacon` IS NOT A VALUE. Sentence (4) makes the name
%     CONDITIONAL — bacon "instead of drapery, when the characteristic
%     layers are present" — so the condition rides inside the atom,
%     following the placement rule `veto-override.adj` and
%     `karst-process-zone.adj` already apply. A query for the unconditional
%     name therefore abstains, because asking for it is asking what a
%     drapery is ALWAYS called, which this sentence declines to say.
%   - The plurals are the source's own. It writes "cascades, rivers,
%     glaciers, or organ pipes", so those are the atoms; singularising
%     them would be a silent edit of the citation.
%   - `stalactite`, `stalagmite`, `helictite`, `flowstone` and `soda_straw`
%     get no rows. This page gives them exactly one name each, and
%     inventing synonyms from general karst vocabulary is precisely what a
%     grounded recall library must not do.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table speleothem_alt_name {
    columns speleothem, alt_name

    row (column, pillar)
    row (coralloid, corallite)
    row (frozen_waterfall, petrified_waterfall)
    row (frozen_waterfall, cascades)
    row (frozen_waterfall, rivers)
    row (frozen_waterfall, glaciers)
    row (frozen_waterfall, organ_pipes)
    row (drapery, cave_bacon_when_characteristic_layers_present)

    source "When a stalagmite grows together with its counterpart feeder stalactite, a new speleothem is formed: a column or pillar."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    trust authoritative
    cites "Coralloid (or corallite or cave popcorn) is a catchall term describing knobby, nodular, botryoidal, or corallike speleothems." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    cites "The most common of these is the petrified or frozen waterfall, also referred to as cascades, rivers, glaciers, or organ pipes." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    cites "Cave Bacon forms on slanted surfaces, and is called \"bacon\" instead of drapery, when the characteristic layers are present." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
}
